TI  - A case-control study of the role of human papillomavirus in oesophageal squamous 
      cell carcinoma in australia.
PG  - 236482
LID - 10.1155/2014/236482 [doi]
LID - 236482
AB  - Objective. We investigate the prevalence of human papillomavirus (HPV) in 
      oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC) tissues compared to oesophageal tissue 
      from healthy controls, in an Australian cohort. Methods. We conducted a 
      hospital-based case-control study of 99 patients with OSCC and 100 healthy 
      controls to examine the presence of HPV DNA. Paraffin tissues were tested using 
      the PapType high-risk HPV detection and genotyping kit and with INNO-LiPA HPV 
      Genotyping Extra. The biopsy samples were tested for HPV using a PCR-ELISA method 
      based on the L1 consensus primer set PGMY09-PGMY11. Results. HPV DNA of the 
      oncogenic genotype 16 was detected in 1/99 case specimens, a rate of 1010 per 
      100,000 (95% CI: 30-5500). All control specimens were negative for HPV. 
      Significantly higher rates of smoking, other aerodigestive cancers, and mortality 
      were seen among cases than controls. A pooled analysis of this study and the only 
      other Australian case-control study found that 9/321 cases and 0/155 controls 
      were positive for HPV. The pooled odds ratio for HPV being a risk factor for OSCC 
      was 9.35 (95% CI: 0.47-190.33). Conclusion. Our results suggest that in this 
      multifactorial cancer HPV may be an additional risk factor; although a larger, 
      better powered study is needed.
