DP  - 2026 Sep 30
TI  - Risk of malignant transformation in oral lichen planus and oral lichen planus 
      with dysplasia: A retrospective clinical study of 435 histologically verified 
      cases.
LID - 10.17219/dmp/209982 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Chronic activity of the subepithelial lymphocytic infiltrate may 
      induce epithelial changes associated with malignant transformation (MT) in oral 
      lichen planus (OLP) cases. OBJECTIVES: This study compared the risk of developing 
      oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC) in a large cohort of patients with OLP and 
      OLP with epithelial dysplasia (OLD). Special attention was paid to carcinoma 
      location and clinical factors, such as desquamative gingivitis and reticular, 
      erosive or ulcerative status. MATERIAL AND METHODS: This retrospective study 
      included 435 histologically verified OLP/OLD cases treated between 2004 and 2024. 
      To assess risk factors, medical history data on medication, smoking and 
      comorbidities was collected. Clinical and pathological aspects were correlated. 
      Statistical analyses were performed using SigmaXL software. RESULTS: Of the cases 
      included in the study, 59.8% were classified as OLP (260/435) and 40.2% as OLD 
      (175/435). A case was considered a true MT only if it followed the OLP/OLD biopsy 
      results. According to this criterion, 30 OSCCs were diagnosed in 21 patients. 
      Oral squamous cell carcinoma developed in 2.3% of OLP cases (6/260) and 8.6% of 
      OLD cases (15/175), with a statistically significant difference between the 
      groups (p = 0.0027). The most common site for OSCC was the gingiva/alveolar 
      mucosa, accounting for 33.3% (10/30) of tumors, followed by the tongue (20.0%, 
      6/30). Two alveolar carcinomas were located at sites of peri-implantitis 
      associated with desquamative gingivitis. In 2 patients, synchronous OSCCs were 
      diagnosed in the maxilla and mandible. CONCLUSIONS: In this study, 8.6% of 
      patients with OLD developed OSCC. Although OLP without dysplasia still carries a 
      risk of OSCC, this risk was approx. 4 times lower, at 2.3%. Cases with 
      desquamative gingivitis require close monitoring due to their higher risk of 
      carcinoma development. We recommend careful observation of desquamative 
      gingivitis around dental implants or in areas of ongoing dental inflammation. 
      Surgical revision with biopsy should be considered if a resorptive process around 
      an implant is suspected.
