TI  - Saliva as a window into tumor immunity in oral squamous cell carcinoma: tissue 
      concordance and translational validation.
PG  - 1957454
LID - 10.3389/fimmu.2026.1957454 [doi]
LID - 1957454
AB  - Saliva is non-invasive, repeatable, and anatomically close to oral squamous cell 
      carcinoma (OSCC), but proximity does not establish that a salivary biomolecule 
      originates from the tumor. Cytokines, extracellular vesicles, coding and 
      non-coding RNAs, and tumor-derived DNA may arise from tumor and immune cells, 
      adjacent mucosa, periodontal tissues, salivary glands, oral microbiota, or 
      systemic circulation. This review evaluates whether salivary immune signals 
      reflect the OSCC tissue immune microenvironment using three evidence levels: 
      quantitative paired tissue-saliva concordance within patients (Level I); indirect 
      clinicopathological, source-enriched, paired-biofluid, or longitudinal support 
      without quantitative paired tissue analysis (Level II); and saliva-only 
      case-control differences (Level III). IL-6, IL-8/CXCL8, IL-1β, and TNF-α show 
      relatively consistent salivary changes, whereas evidence for IL-10, selected 
      chemokines, extracellular vesicle-associated PD-L1, and secreted miRNAs or 
      lncRNAs remains limited or inconsistent. Salivary tumor DNA can show strong 
      tissue-genotype concordance, but reflects tumor-derived material rather than 
      immune state. Most immune candidates remain at Level II or III; none robustly 
      meets Level I. Periodontitis, mucositis, infection, smoking, tumor ulceration, 
      salivary flow, and sample processing complicate interpretation. We propose paired 
      collection of tumor, adjacent mucosa, saliva, and plasma; spatial 
      immunophenotyping; oral-inflammation controls; longitudinal perioperative and 
      treatment sampling; standardized assays; and multicenter validation. The 
      framework prioritizes biological traceability over diagnostic discrimination. At 
      present, saliva is best considered a complementary longitudinal biosample, not a 
      substitute for tissue immunophenotyping.
